1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:04,320
Module four, video 4.6: Refused, Not Worked Around.

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:09,440
Uh, so imagine it is two days before a deadline in your ministry.

3
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:10,980
Right, the pressure's really on.

4
00:00:11,180 --> 00:00:16,219
Exactly. A program stops your supplier's build, and the supplier asks for, you know, one favor.

5
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:18,800
They say, "Switch the check off, just this once."

6
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:20,460
Yeah, that happens a lot.

7
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:27,020
So why must your program refuse a supplier's build that contradicts accepted designs rather than just working around it?

8
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:32,100
Well, because the answer should already be written down, and it is firmly no.

9
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:33,520
No exceptions. None.

10
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:40,440
To understand why, uh, we need to look at exactly what the program checks before anything is generated, built, or installed.

11
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:45,620
Okay, so when the program reads the file, what are the four questions it asks to verify the build?

12
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:48,500
The program asks exactly four questions.

13
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:49,140
Number one:

14
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:52,740
Does the file name the interaction design you accepted?

15
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:57,860
Mm-hmm. Number two: Does the document say it was accepted by a name on a date?

16
00:00:58,460 --> 00:01:03,120
Correct. Number three: Is it the same document, unchanged since it was accepted?

17
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:09,140
And, uh, number four: Does every list, search, move, and act match what the document decided?

18
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:13,240
Wow, okay. Yeah, and if any answer is no, the build stops.

19
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:22,540
But, I mean, if an answer is no and the build stops but there is a massive deadline looming for your minister, surely there is a back-end setting to bypass this.

20
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:27,400
No. There is no switch, uh, no setting, and no exception for deadlines.

21
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:30,360
Wait, really? Not even for a critical deadline?

22
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:31,280
Not even then.

23
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:33,920
Hmm. The deadline does not change the agreement.

24
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:36,880
You know, it only changes how fast the correction is needed.

25
00:01:37,480 --> 00:01:38,140
Ah, I see.

26
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:43,900
Because if a system allows itself to be bypassed, your digital infrastructure becomes fragmented.

27
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,640
It gets siloed, locked into one supplier, and costly to change.

28
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:49,340
Right. That makes total sense.

29
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:56,200
So if a refusal must be answered and not worked around, what are the two honest answers you can give to move forward?

30
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:58,860
Well, there are two honest answers here.

31
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:02,900
Number one: Correct the file to match exactly what you accepted.

32
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:04,620
Okay. And the other...

33
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:11,200
Or number two: The owner accepts a revised interaction design, and the file names that new version.

34
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:15,220
So either way, both options leave a clear record of who decided what.

35
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:16,440
Exactly.

36
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:22,180
Whereas, you know, switching a check off leaves no record, and the exact same fault will return at the next build.

37
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:24,140
Yeah, you definitely want to avoid that loop.

38
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:30,480
So let us look at, at a storyboard demonstration of what these runs will show in practice using Progressa.

39
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:33,640
Right. Progressa, the fictional country of these examples.

40
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:34,040
Yes.

41
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:41,940
So in the Ministry of Education, a director accepted a design stating that 20 matters PHEQA advises on

42
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:44,260
must be categorized into three groups.

43
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:47,080
Just three groups, yeah. Right. Eight, seven, and five.

44
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:47,500
Right.

45
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:50,840
So how does the program handle the file when it is submitted?

46
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:57,920
So the recording will show two runs. In the first run, the file offers all 20 matters in one single list.

47
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:00,280
Oh, so they completely miss the categories.

48
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:06,500
Exactly. The program refuses it and explicitly names the document and the exact row it disagrees with.

49
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:08,820
Which means nobody has to guess what to fix.

50
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:10,440
Right. It is totally clear.

51
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:15,940
Okay, so after that initial refusal, what happens in the second run once the supplier makes the correction?

52
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:17,640
Well, the correction is really small.

53
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:20,860
The list is given the three categories. Mm-hmm.

54
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:22,020
The program reads it again,

55
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:24,260
and this time it is admitted.

56
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:28,320
The recording will show the first run refused with the row named,

57
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:30,640
then admitted after the correction.

58
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:36,360
So when your program refuses a build, what is the exact procedure your officials must follow?

59
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:41,020
When your program refuses a build, do not ask who can switch it off.

60
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:47,120
Ask which row it names, then either correct the file or accept a revised decision.
